// src/kernel/core/cap/cap_net_privileged.pdx -- R100-PREP-003 (paideia-os #2009)
//
// R_NET_PRIVILEGED_PROTOCOL -- kernel-side elevate-class gate for
// syscalls that inject raw L3/L4 protocol frames onto the wire (or
// through the kernel-internal loopback path that shortcuts the same
// codepath). At this landing the only consumer is sys_icmp_echo
// (sysno 103, R100-PREP-003); future consumers include a raw-socket
// send primitive when one lands.
//
// This file is deliberately narrow -- it declares ONE right constant
// and ONE check function. It does NOT introduce a per-task capability
// bitmap: none exists in the tree yet, and inventing one for a single
// consumer would be a cross-cutting change out of scope for a PREP
// landing. When the userspace libpdx-elevate policy class for
// R_NET_PRIVILEGED_PROTOCOL lands (out of scope until libpdx-elevate
// wires its M2 dispatch through svc.elevate-broker per
// design/tooling/r49-r50-plan.md §5), this file's check function
// becomes a delegation into the elevate-broker RPC path; the constant
// stays byte-identical so consumers do not need to rewire.
//
// GATE POLICY (this landing):
//   cap_check_r_net_privileged_protocol(task_ptr) -> 1 (allow) iff
//     * task_ptr == 0 (kernel/boot context -- no ring-3 caller), OR
//     * task_ptr's pid (u32 @ TCB+0, TASK_OFF_PID) == 1 (init)
//   -> 0 (deny) otherwise.
//
// This matches the R73 sys_kill "MVP is fail-closed" posture (SIGSTOP/
// SIGCONT deliver only, everything else -EINVAL) -- the check permits
// exactly the two callers the boot witness and the future userspace
// ping tool will use, and rejects every other ring-3 task with -EPERM.
//
// FRAME PROLOGUE: none. This is a leaf function using manual push/pop
// discipline (same as every other kind_*.pdx helper in this dir). Frame
// prologue implicit (compiler suppresses it for unsafe-bodied lambdas).
//
// Reserved-label discipline: labels prefixed `cnpp_` (cap net priv
// protocol). `loop`, `if` etc. are keywords and never used as labels.

module CapNetPrivileged = structure {

  // Right constant. Assigned bit 12 (0x1000). No other right in the
  // tree currently claims 0x1000 -- verified by grep of src/kernel/**
  // at landing time (the R_* bitmask constants that DO exist all sit
  // in either the low 8 bits for IPC/generic rights, or the 0x100..
  // 0x400 range for kind-specific rights per kind_tls_trust.pdx and
  // kind_tui_canvas.pdx). Bit 12 leaves 0x800 free for a future
  // R_NET_UNPRIVILEGED_PROTOCOL (design-obvious sibling) without
  // reshuffling this file.
  pub let R_NET_PRIVILEGED_PROTOCOL : u64 = 0x1000

  // cap_check_r_net_privileged_protocol(task_ptr) -> u64
  //
  // SysV: rdi = task_ptr (TCB pointer or 0 for kernel/boot context)
  //    -> rax = 1 (allow) | 0 (deny)
  //
  // Leaf function; no callee-save writes (rax/rcx/rdx all caller-save).
  pub let cap_check_r_net_privileged_protocol : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{cap} =
    fn (task_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "R100-PREP-003 (paideia-os #2009): R_NET_PRIVILEGED_PROTOCOL gate. Two arms per file-header GATE POLICY. Arm 1: task_ptr == 0 -> allow (kernel/boot context -- the boot witness at r100_prep_003_icmp_echo.pdx calls sys_icmp_echo_body directly WITHOUT going through the dispatch shim, so _current_tcb has not been set to the calling task; letting task_ptr==0 through is what lets the boot witness exercise the ICMP echo path end-to-end without a fake TCB). Arm 2: task_ptr != 0 -> load pid (u32 @ TCB+0, TASK_OFF_PID -- established by every existing TCB reader in the tree, see sched/task_pool.pdx L782) and allow iff pid == 1 (init). This is the R73 fail-closed posture applied to networking: exactly the two callers we know about today are permitted, every other ring-3 task is refused with -EPERM by the caller. When userspace libpdx-elevate lands its M2 dispatch through svc.elevate-broker (design/tooling/r49-r50-plan.md §5), this function grows an elevate-broker query; today the broker's dispatch stub returns DENY unconditionally (elevate_broker.pdx), so gating on it here would break the boot witness. Effects {mem} for the TCB load; capabilities {cap} matches every other cap-check helper's posture. Encoder-conservative: cmp/je/mov/xor/ret only -- no new addressing mode. Leaf; no callee-save prologue. Labels prefixed cnpp_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  cnpp_allow;                  // Arm 1: kernel/boot context

        // Arm 2: load pid (u32 @ TCB+0) into eax (zero-extends to rax).
        mov eax, [rdi + 0];
        cmp rax, 1;                      // init pid
        jne cnpp_deny;

      cnpp_allow:
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;

      cnpp_deny:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }
}
